---
import { Picture } from 'astro:assets';
import testimonial_lionel from '../../assets/ebook/lionel.png';
import testimonial_utsav from '../../assets/ebook/utsav.png';
import testimonial_pierre from '../../assets/ebook/pierre.png';
import testimonial_virgile from '../../assets/ebook/virgile.png';
import testimonial_paul from '../../assets/ebook/paul.png';
import testimonial_thomas from '../../assets/ebook/thomas.png';
import testimonial_cedric from '../../assets/ebook/cedric.png';
import testimonial_joseph from '../../assets/ebook/joseph.png';
import TestimonialWebsiteLink from './TestimonialWebsiteLink.astro';
---

<section class="testimonials">
	<h2 class="testimonials__title">Loved by the community</h2>
	<fieldset class="switcher">
		<legend class="visually-hidden">Testimonial layout</legend>
		<label class="switcher__option" for="two-columns">
			<input type="radio" name="testimonial-columns" value="2" id="two-columns" checked />
			<span>2 columns</span>
		</label>

		<label class="switcher__option" for="three-columns">
			<input type="radio" name="testimonial-columns" value="3" id="three-columns" />
			<span>3 columns</span>
		</label>
	</fieldset>

	<div class="testimonials__container testimonials__reviews">
		<div class="heart heart-1"></div>
		<ul class="testimonial__list">
			<li class="testimonial">
				<div class="testimonial__content">
					<Picture
						src={testimonial_utsav}
						formats={['avif', 'webp', 'jpg']}
						width="168"
						height="168"
						alt=""
						class="testimonial__image"
					/>
					<p class="testimonial__author">
						Utsav Meena
						<TestimonialWebsiteLink href="https://flexicajourney.com/" label="Visit Utsav Meena’s website" />
						<br />
						<span>CSS Content Creator</span>
					</p>
				</div>
				<blockquote class="testimonial__text">
					It’s an easy-to-follow guide with everything explained step by step. I also liked the extra pointers on
					accessibility, troubleshooting, and browser compatibility. <br />Hopefully, this will help people appreciate
					CSS a bit more.
				</blockquote>
			</li>

			<li class="testimonial">
				<div class="testimonial__content">
					<Picture
						src={testimonial_lionel}
						formats={['avif', 'webp', 'jpg']}
						width="168"
						height="168"
						alt=""
						class="testimonial__image"
					/>
					<p class="testimonial__author">
						Lionel Péramo
						<TestimonialWebsiteLink href="http://lionel-peramo.com/" label="Visit Lionel Péramo’s website" />
						<br />
						<span>Fullstack Developer & Content Creator</span>
					</p>
				</div>
				<blockquote class="testimonial__text">
					Theo brilliantly shows how modern CSS can replace complex JavaScript without losing creativity or
					functionality. The examples are clear, practical, and inspiring. A refreshing reminder that the simplest tools
					often create the best results. A must-read for any developer.
				</blockquote>
			</li>

			<li class="testimonial">
				<div class="testimonial__content">
					<Picture
						src={testimonial_pierre}
						formats={['avif', 'webp', 'jpg']}
						width="168"
						height="168"
						alt=""
						class="testimonial__image"
					/>
					<p class="testimonial__author">Pierre Liegard<br /> <span>Front-end Developer</span></p>
				</div>
				<blockquote class="testimonial__text">
					This book offers a welcome reminder of the latest CSS developments at a time when performance matters more
					than ever.
				</blockquote>
			</li>

			<li class="testimonial">
				<div class="testimonial__content">
					<Picture
						src={testimonial_cedric}
						formats={['avif', 'webp', 'jpg']}
						width="168"
						height="168"
						alt=""
						class="testimonial__image"
					/>
					<p class="testimonial__author">Cedric Molla<br /> <span>Web Developer</span></p>
				</div>
				<blockquote class="testimonial__text">
					Such a great read! It’s practical, easy to follow, and packed with smart ideas I can actually use. It made me
					want to revisit old projects and try a lighter, CSS-first approach.
				</blockquote>
			</li>

			<li class="testimonial">
				<div class="testimonial__content">
					<Picture
						src={testimonial_virgile}
						formats={['avif', 'webp', 'jpg']}
						width="168"
						height="168"
						alt=""
						class="testimonial__image"
					/>
					<p class="testimonial__author">
						Virgile Gouala
						<TestimonialWebsiteLink href="https://goua.la/" label="Visit Virgile Gouala’s website" />
						<br />
						<span>Engineering Manager</span>
					</p>
				</div>
				<blockquote class="testimonial__text">
					<i>You Don’t Need JavaScript</i> introduced me to the “Rule of Least Power”, a principle that is both simple and
					profoundly enlightening. In a web world that’s always accelerating, this work promotes a more ecological, thoughtful,
					and handcrafted approach to development, reminding us that elegance and impact often arise from simplicity.
				</blockquote>
			</li>
			<li class="testimonial">
				<div class="testimonial__content">
					<Picture
						src={testimonial_paul}
						formats={['avif', 'webp', 'jpg']}
						width="168"
						height="168"
						alt=""
						class="testimonial__image"
					/>
					<p class="testimonial__author">
						Paul Wright
						<TestimonialWebsiteLink href="https://www.suedeapple.co.uk/" label="Visit Paul Wright’s website" />
						<br />
						<span>Full Stack Web Developer</span>
					</p>
				</div>
				<blockquote class="testimonial__text">
					Read this book and you’ll be gleefully ripping out half your JS before you’ve even finished the second
					chapter. <i>You Don’t Need JavaScript</i> is a breath of fresh air packed with “wait… CSS can do that?!” moments.
					Theo has a knack for making complex ideas feel effortless, proving that elegant, interactive design doesn’t always
					need a mountain of JS. It’s the perfect reminder that simplicity, speed, and creativity often live right there in
					your stylesheet.
				</blockquote>
			</li>
			<li class="testimonial">
				<div class="testimonial__content">
					<Picture
						src={testimonial_thomas}
						formats={['avif', 'webp', 'jpg']}
						width="168"
						height="168"
						alt=""
						class="testimonial__image"
					/>
					<p class="testimonial__author">
						Thomas Palef
						<TestimonialWebsiteLink href="https://www.lessmilk.com/" label="Visit Thomas Palef’s website" />
						<br />
						<span>Author of Make 2D Games</span>
					</p>
				</div>
				<blockquote class="testimonial__text">
					I thought I knew CSS, but I was wrong... this book taught me new CSS properties at almost every page!
				</blockquote>
			</li>

			<li class="testimonial">
				<div class="testimonial__content">
					<Picture
						src={testimonial_joseph}
						formats={['avif', 'webp', 'jpg']}
						width="168"
						height="168"
						alt=""
						class="testimonial__image"
					/>
					<p class="testimonial__author">
						Joseph
						<TestimonialWebsiteLink
							href="https://acworthwebdesigns.com/"
							label="Visit Joseph’s Acworth Web Designs website"
						/>
						<br />
						<span>Acworth Web Designs LLC</span>
					</p>
				</div>
				<blockquote class="testimonial__text">
					An important read for web designers in this era. Theo takes some of the most powerful new web features of the
					last few years &amp; distills them into simple, immediately-useful examples. If you're like me, you'll be able
					to significantly reduce your JavaScript footprint overnight, while also improving the quality of your web
					pages.
				</blockquote>
			</li>
		</ul>
		<div class="heart heart-2"></div>
	</div>
</section>
